  The two issues I did find came from my own self-review and are already fixed in the spec: the browser global names
  were unspecified (now [38;5;153mwindow.__loggerCore[39m and [38;5;153mwindow.logger[39m), and it was ambiguous whether loading an adapter installs
  the error handlers (it does, as a side effect, with [38;5;153mconfigureRemote[39m the only explicit call).

  ---

  Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-17-logging-design.md[39m (not committed). Please review it and let me know
  if you want changes before I write the implementation plan.

  Two things in it worth your attention specifically, since they're the ones that bind hardest later:

  - [1mThe record model is the wire format.[0m [38;5;153m{ v, ts, level, msg, ctx, err, runtime, sessionId }[39m is what a receiving
    endpoint will parse, and changing it after records are in a log store is the expensive kind of change. Now is the
    cheap moment to object.
  - [1m[38;5;153mREMOTE_CTX_ALLOWLIST[39m starts at [38;5;153mevent[39m, [38;5;153mreason[39m, [38;5;153msuccess[39m, [38;5;153mhttpStatus[39m, [38;5;153mdurationMs[39m.[0m Notably absent: [38;5;153musername[39m. That means
    your remote records will tell you a login failed but not whose — deliberate, and reversible by adding one string,
    but you should decide it consciously rather than discover it during an incident.

